# Tessera stdlib: MemoryManager pattern and memory routines
#
# Every memory region a program uses has a manager, which lends blocks of it and takes them back. A heap's manager obeys
# HeapManageable (blocks come back in any order), and a page manager obeys PageManageable (it also reserves address
# space and sets what pages allow). Both refine MemoryManageable, the protocol of a manager that lends blocks (borrow,
# reborrow, give_back). m.to[MemoryManager]() makes the MemoryManager table that callers pass as @MemoryManager to any
# routine that allocates: routines that forward to m's own, with m's address as the table's state. Nothing here needs an
# operating system. On a target with one, DefaultMemory.te has the hosted tables (HEAP, C_HEAP, PAGES), whose managers
# are Heap.te's and Pages.te's. A stack is a StackManager's (StackManager.te): it hands out a routine's frame slots by
# moving the stack pointer, with no call, so it has no table. A MemoryManager also obeys ResourceManageable, the
# protocol behind `x: &T <- value from m`.
#
# Heap memory reaches user code as a BorrowedSlice[T] (BorrowedSlice.te in System) or a BorrowedCell[T]:
# an address, a count, and this manager, released with drop(). allocate,
# reallocate, and deallocate below are the raw layer under it, for the stdlib's
# own node-based structures (B-tree nodes, thread and fiber records) and for
# memory that C code hands over. Sizes and counts are U64.
#
# With the heap check on (config.toml's [debug] heap-check), reallocate and
# deallocate note the place they were called from while the memory manager runs, so
# a memory manager that finds a mistake (the checked heap's check for a block given
# back twice) can crash at the caller's line: memory_manager_call_place() reads it.

module System/Memory

# ============================================================
# MemoryManager record
#
# Callbacks use the C calling convention and U64 sizes, so libc's own
# functions fit behind them.
# ============================================================

### What a page allows: `NONE` (touching it stops the program), `READ`, or `READ_WRITE`.
choice PageAccess: U8
    NONE
    READ
    READ_WRITE

### The table of a memory manager that allocating routines take as `@MemoryManager`, so no allocation is hidden. A
### manager makes it with `m.to[MemoryManager]()`: routines that call the manager's own, and the manager's address. The
### page routines (`reserve`, `commit`, `release`, `protect`) are there when the manager obeys `PageManageable`, and
### calling one that isn't crashes. The table views the manager: the manager outlives it, which the build checks
### inside a routine.
entity MemoryManager
    ### Lends `size` bytes aligned to `align` (a power of two), or returns null: `(state, size, align)`.
    secret borrow_fn: Callable[(Addr, U64, U64), Addr]
    ### Resizes a block to `size` bytes, keeping its contents and its alignment `align`, or returns null and leaves it
    ### as it was: `(state, at, size, align)`.
    secret reborrow_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64, U64), Addr]
    ### Takes a block back: `(state, at)`.
    secret give_back_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr), None]
    ### Reserves `size` bytes of address space, no page usable yet, or returns null: `(state, size)`.
    secret reserve_fn: Callable[(Addr, U64), Addr]
    ### Makes reserved pages readable and writable, false when it can't: `(state, at, size)`.
    secret commit_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64), Bool]
    ### Gives a whole reservation back: `(state, at, size)` with `at` and `size` as reserved.
    secret release_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64), None]
    ### Sets what pages allow, false when it can't: `(state, at, size, access)`.
    secret protect_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64, PageAccess), Bool]
    ### What the routines share, passed to each of them: the manager's address.
    secret state: Addr

### A memory manager had no memory to give: the memory manager returned null. What asked for it is left as it was (a
### collection keeps its elements).
crashable OutOfMemoryError
    nothing

### A manager that lends a place and puts a value in it: what a `<-` line with `from` stands for. `x: &T <- value from
### m` is `m.borrow_and_fill[T](t: value)`, and `drop x` is `x.destruct()`, which gives the place back to the manager
### the `&T` holds. A place from STACK is a frame slot the builder makes, with no call.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
protocol ResourceManageable[M]
    ### Borrows a place for one `T` and puts `t` in it.
    ### :typeparam T: The value's type.
    ### :param t: The value, taken over.
    ### :returns: The place, which `drop` gives back to this manager.
    routine @Me.borrow_and_fill[T](t: T) -> BorrowedCell[T]

### A type that lends memory: it lends blocks (`borrow`), resizes them (`reborrow`), and takes them back (`give_back`). A value of it in a slot
### becomes the table every allocating routine takes with `m.to[MemoryManager]()`.
### :typeparam T: The manager type.
protocol MemoryManageable[T]
    ### Lends `size` bytes aligned to `align` (a power of two), or returns null when it can't.
    ### :param size: The number of bytes.
    ### :param align: The alignment, a power of two.
    ### :returns: The block, or null.
    routine @Me.borrow(size: U64, align: U64) -> Addr

    ### Resizes the block at `at` to `size` bytes, keeping its contents up to the smaller size and its alignment `align`.
    ### Only the manager knows how large a block is, so every manager has its own.
    ### :param at: The block, from this manager, or null for a new one.
    ### :param size: The new size in bytes.
    ### :param align: The alignment the block was borrowed with, a power of two.
    ### :returns: The resized block, which may have moved, or null with the block left as it was when it can't.
    ### :note: A manager whose reborrow calls another manager's reborrow on `at` marks it `@reshaping(at)`.
    routine @Me.reborrow(at: Addr, size: U64, align: U64) -> Addr

    ### Takes the block at `at` back.
    ### :param at: The block, from this manager. Null does nothing in the stdlib's managers.
    routine @Me.give_back(at: Addr) -> None

### A memory manager of a heap: its blocks come back in any order, each one on its own. It asks for nothing more than
### `MemoryManageable`: obeying it says the manager takes its blocks back in any order. `HeapManagerByC` and
### `HeapManagerByCheckedHeap` (Heap.te) are two, and `HEAP` is the default one.
### :typeparam T: The manager type.
protocol HeapManageable[T]
obeys MemoryManageable[T]

### A memory manager that also handles pages: it reserves address space, commits pages of it, gives a reservation back,
### and sets what pages allow. Its table's `has_pages()` and `has_protect()` are true. A manager whose pages can't
### change what they allow returns false from `protect`.
### :typeparam T: The manager type.
protocol PageManageable[T]
obeys MemoryManageable[T]
    ### Reserves `size` bytes of address space, no page of it usable yet.
    ### :param size: The number of bytes, rounded up to whole pages by the manager.
    ### :returns: The reservation's start, or null when the space can't be reserved.
    routine @Me.reserve(size: U64) -> Addr

    ### Makes `size` bytes of reserved pages at `at` readable and writable.
    ### :param at: The first page, page aligned, inside a reservation of this manager.
    ### :param size: The number of bytes, a whole number of pages.
    ### :returns: False when the pages can't be committed.
    routine @Me.commit(at: Addr, size: U64) -> Bool

    ### Gives a whole reservation back, committed pages and all.
    ### :param at: The reservation's start, as `reserve` returned it.
    ### :param size: The size `reserve` was asked for.
    routine @Me.release(at: Addr, size: U64) -> None

    ### Sets what `size` bytes of pages at `at` allow.
    ### :param at: The first page, page aligned.
    ### :param size: The number of bytes, a whole number of pages.
    ### :param access: What the pages allow.
    ### :returns: False when the access can't be set.
    routine @Me.protect(at: Addr, size: U64, access: PageAccess) -> Bool

obeys ResourceManageable[MemoryManager]

### `t` in memory borrowed from this manager: what `x: &T <- t from m` stands for, given back with `drop x`.
### :typeparam T: The value's type.
### :param t: The value, taken over.
### :returns: The place.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the manager has no memory to give.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates memory")
routine @MemoryManager.borrow_and_fill[T](t: T) -> BorrowedCell[T]
needs AnyType T
    return BorrowedCell[T].construct(value: steal t, manager: me)

### Writes `Failed to allocate memory.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine OutOfMemoryError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter obeys Writer
    out.write("Failed to allocate memory.")
    return

### A table that hands out blocks and doesn't handle pages: its page routines and `protect_fn` are null. For
### System/BorrowedSlice's own tables built from C routines, everything else makes its table with `m.to[MemoryManager]()`.
### :param borrow_fn: Allocates `(state, size, align)`.
### :param reborrow_fn: Resizes `(state, at, size, align)`.
### :param give_back_fn: Gives a block back `(state, at)`.
### :param state: What the three routines share.
### :returns: The table.
secret common routine MemoryManager.construct(borrow_fn: Callable[(Addr, U64, U64), Addr],
    reborrow_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64, U64), Addr], give_back_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr), None],
    state: Addr) -> Me
    return Me(borrow_fn, reborrow_fn, give_back_fn, reserve_fn: .hollow(), commit_fn: .hollow(),
        release_fn: .hollow(), protect_fn: .hollow(), state)

### The table of a manager that hands out blocks and doesn't handle pages: routines that call its `borrow`, `reborrow`,
### and `give_back`, with its address. Its page routines are empty (`has_pages()` and `has_protect()` are false).
### :typeparam T: The manager type.
### :returns: The table, which views the manager: the manager outlives every use of it.
routine @T.to[MemoryManager]() -> MemoryManager
needs AnyType T, T obeys MemoryManageable
    return MemoryManager(borrow_fn: forward_borrow[T].to[Callable](),
        reborrow_fn: forward_reborrow[T].to[Callable](),
        give_back_fn: forward_give_back[T].to[Callable](), reserve_fn: .hollow(),
        commit_fn: .hollow(), release_fn: .hollow(), protect_fn: .hollow(), state: me)

### The table of a manager that handles pages: routines that call its `borrow`, `reborrow`, `give_back`, `reserve`, `commit`,
### `release`, and `protect`, with its address (`has_pages()` and `has_protect()` are true).
### :typeparam T: The manager type.
### :returns: The table, which views the manager: the manager outlives every use of it.
routine @T.to[MemoryManager]() -> MemoryManager
needs AnyType T, T obeys PageManageable
    return MemoryManager(borrow_fn: forward_borrow[T].to[Callable](),
        reborrow_fn: forward_reborrow[T].to[Callable](),
        give_back_fn: forward_give_back[T].to[Callable](),
        reserve_fn: forward_reserve[T].to[Callable](), commit_fn: forward_commit[T].to[Callable](),
        release_fn: forward_release[T].to[Callable](),
        protect_fn: forward_protect[T].to[Callable](), state: me)

# The table's routines: each turns the state back into the manager it came from and calls the manager's own. This is
# the one place a table's state is read as a manager.

### Calls the manager's `borrow`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param size: The number of bytes.
### :param align: The alignment, a power of two.
### :returns: The block, or null.
secret routine forward_borrow[M](state: Addr, size: U64, align: U64) -> Addr
needs M obeys MemoryManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    return manager.borrow(size, align)

### Calls the manager's `reborrow`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param at: The block, or null.
### :param size: The new size in bytes.
### :param align: The block's alignment.
### :returns: The resized block, or null with the block left as it was.
@reshaping(at)
secret routine forward_reborrow[M](state: Addr, at: Addr, size: U64, align: U64) -> Addr
needs M obeys MemoryManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    return manager.reborrow(at, size, align)

### Calls the manager's `give_back`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param at: The block.
secret routine forward_give_back[M](state: Addr, at: Addr) -> None
needs M obeys MemoryManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    manager.give_back(at)
    return

### Calls the manager's `reserve`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param size: The number of bytes.
### :returns: The reservation's start, or null.
secret routine forward_reserve[M](state: Addr, size: U64) -> Addr
needs M obeys PageManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    return manager.reserve(size)

### Calls the manager's `commit`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param at: The first page.
### :param size: The number of bytes.
### :returns: False when the pages can't be committed.
secret routine forward_commit[M](state: Addr, at: Addr, size: U64) -> Bool
needs M obeys PageManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    return manager.commit(at, size)

### Calls the manager's `release`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param at: The reservation's start.
### :param size: The size reserved.
secret routine forward_release[M](state: Addr, at: Addr, size: U64) -> None
needs M obeys PageManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    manager.release(at, size)
    return

### Calls the manager's `protect`.
### :typeparam M: The manager type.
### :param state: The manager's address.
### :param at: The first page.
### :param size: The number of bytes.
### :param access: What the pages allow.
### :returns: False when the access can't be set.
secret routine forward_protect[M](state: Addr, at: Addr, size: U64, access: PageAccess) -> Bool
needs M obeys PageManageable
    manager: @M = state.to[@M]()
    return manager.protect(at, size, access)

### Borrows `size` bytes aligned to `align` from this manager, to give back with `give_back`.
### :param size: The number of bytes.
### :param align: The alignment, a power of two.
### :returns: The block, or null when the manager has no memory to give.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when `align` isn't a power of two.
@track_caller
@expensive("borrows memory")
routine @MemoryManager.borrow(size: U64, align: U64) -> Addr
    check_align(align)
    borrow_fn: Callable[(Addr, U64, U64), Addr] = me.borrow_fn.peek()
    block_start: Addr = borrow_fn.call(me.state.peek(), size, align)
    return block_start

### Resizes the block at `at`, from this manager, to `size` bytes, keeping its contents up to the smaller size.
### :param at: The block, or null for a new one.
### :param size: The new size in bytes.
### :param align: The alignment the block was borrowed with, a power of two.
### :returns: The resized block, which may have moved, or null when the manager has no memory to give (the block is
###     left as it was).
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when `align` isn't a power of two.
@track_caller
@expensive("borrows memory again")
@reshaping(at)
routine @MemoryManager.reborrow(at: Addr, size: U64, align: U64) -> Addr
    check_align(align)
    reborrow_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64, U64), Addr] = me.reborrow_fn.peek()
    note_call_place(caller_location())
    block_start: Addr = reborrow_fn.call(me.state.peek(), at, size, align)
    note_call_place(.hollow())
    return block_start

### Gives the block at `at` back to this manager, the one it was borrowed from. The manager takes it back.
### :param at: The block. Null does nothing in the stdlib's managers.
@track_caller
@expensive("gives memory back")
routine @MemoryManager.give_back(at: Addr) -> None
    give_back_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr), None] = me.give_back_fn.peek()
    note_call_place(caller_location())
    give_back_fn.call(me.state.peek(), at)
    note_call_place(.hollow())
    return

### Whether this manager handles pages: `reserve`, `commit`, and `release` are there.
### :returns: True when its page routines are filled in.
routine @MemoryManager.has_pages() -> Bool
    reserve_fn: Callable[(Addr, U64), Addr] = me.reserve_fn.peek()
    return reserve_fn.addr().is_not_hollow()

### Whether this manager can set what pages allow: `protect` is there.
### :returns: True when its protect routine is filled in.
routine @MemoryManager.has_protect() -> Bool
    protect_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64, PageAccess), Bool] = me.protect_fn.peek()
    return protect_fn.addr().is_not_hollow()

### Reserves `size` bytes of address space: whole pages, none of them usable until `commit`.
### :param size: The number of bytes, rounded up to whole pages by the manager.
### :returns: The reservation's start, or null when the space can't be reserved.
### :throws: Crashes with `NotImplementedError` when this manager doesn't handle pages.
@track_caller
@expensive("asks for address space")
routine @MemoryManager.reserve(size: U64) -> Addr
    reserve_fn: Callable[(Addr, U64), Addr] = me.reserve_fn.peek()
    if reserve_fn.addr().is_hollow()
        crash_no_pages()
    at: Addr = reserve_fn.call(me.state.peek(), size)
    return at

### Makes `size` bytes of reserved pages at `at` readable and writable.
### :param at: The first page, page aligned, inside a reservation of this manager.
### :param size: The number of bytes, a whole number of pages.
### :returns: False when the pages can't be committed (no memory left).
### :throws: Crashes with `NotImplementedError` when this manager doesn't handle pages.
@track_caller
@expensive("asks for memory")
routine @MemoryManager.commit(at: Addr, size: U64) -> Bool
    commit_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64), Bool] = me.commit_fn.peek()
    if commit_fn.addr().is_hollow()
        crash_no_pages()
    return commit_fn.call(me.state.peek(), at, size)

### Gives a whole reservation back, committed pages and all.
### :param at: The reservation's start, as `reserve` returned it.
### :param size: The size `reserve` was asked for.
### :throws: Crashes with `NotImplementedError` when this manager doesn't handle pages.
@track_caller
@expensive("gives memory back")
routine @MemoryManager.release(at: Addr, size: U64) -> None
    release_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64), None] = me.release_fn.peek()
    if release_fn.addr().is_hollow()
        crash_no_pages()
    release_fn.call(me.state.peek(), at, size)
    return

### Sets what `size` bytes of pages at `at` allow: a page closed with `PageAccess.NONE` stops the program when touched
### (a guard page).
### :param at: The first page, page aligned.
### :param size: The number of bytes, a whole number of pages.
### :param access: What the pages allow.
### :returns: False when the access can't be set.
### :throws: Crashes with `NotImplementedError` when this manager can't set what pages allow.
@track_caller
@expensive("asks the operating system")
routine @MemoryManager.protect(at: Addr, size: U64, access: PageAccess) -> Bool
    protect_fn: Callable[(Addr, Addr, U64, PageAccess), Bool] = me.protect_fn.peek()
    if protect_fn.addr().is_hollow()
        throw NotImplementedError(description: "This memory manager can't set what pages allow: its protect is empty.")
    return protect_fn.call(me.state.peek(), at, size, access)

### Crashes with `NotImplementedError`: the manager's page routines are empty.
@noreturn
@track_caller
secret routine crash_no_pages() -> None
    throw NotImplementedError(description: "This memory manager doesn't handle pages: its reserve, commit, and release are empty.")

### Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` unless `align` is a power of two.
### :param align: The alignment asked for.
@track_caller
secret routine check_align(align: U64) -> None
    if align.is_power_of_two()
        return
    throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "An alignment must be a power of two.")

# ============================================================
# Generic memory routines (prelude surface)
#
# Sizes and counts are U64, the same type the memory manager callbacks and libc
# take.
# ============================================================

### Allocates room for `count` values of `T` through `manager` and returns a pointer to it, uninitialized.
### :typeparam T: The element type to allocate room for.
### :param count: The number of values of `T` to make room for.
### :param manager: The memory manager to use.
### :returns: A pointer to the new block, or a null pointer when the memory manager fails.
### :throws: Crashes with `IntegerOverflowError` when the byte size does not fit in U64.
### :note: The byte size is `sizeof[T]() * count` with checked multiplication. The memory is uninitialized, and a failed allocation returns a null pointer rather than crashing.
@expensive("allocates heap memory")
routine allocate[T](count: U64, manager: @MemoryManager) -> @T
    elem_size: U64 = sizeof[T]()
    size: U64 = elem_size * count
    raw: Addr = manager.borrow(size, align: alignof[T]())
    typed: @T = raw.to[@T]()
    return typed

### Resizes the block at `ptr` to hold `new_count` values of `T` through `manager` and returns the possibly moved pointer.
### :typeparam T: The element type.
### :param ptr: The block to resize, from `allocate` or an earlier `reallocate`.
### :param new_count: The new number of values of `T`.
### :param manager: The memory manager to use.
### :returns: A pointer to the resized block, which may have moved, or a null pointer when the memory manager fails.
### :throws: Crashes with `IntegerOverflowError` when the byte size does not fit in U64.
### :note: A failed reallocation returns a null pointer, and the old block stays valid.
@track_caller
@expensive("reallocates heap memory")
@reshaping(ptr)
routine reallocate[T](ptr: @T, new_count: U64, manager: @MemoryManager) -> @T
    elem_size: U64 = sizeof[T]()
    size: U64 = elem_size * new_count
    raw: Addr = manager.reborrow(at: ptr, size, align: alignof[T]())
    typed: @T = raw.to[@T]()
    return typed

### Hands the block at `data` back to `manager`, the memory manager it came from. Not a destructor: whatever the values own is
### not released.
### :typeparam T: The element type.
### :param data: The block, from `allocate` or `reallocate`.
### :param manager: The memory manager the block came from.
### :note: A raw layer beside {BorrowedSlice[T]} and {BorrowedCell[T]}, for node-based structures and memory C hands over. Code that holds a BorrowedSlice never needs it.
@track_caller
@expensive("frees heap memory")
routine deallocate[T](data: @T, manager: @MemoryManager) -> None
    manager.give_back(data)
    return

# -- the place of the memory manager call in progress --------------------------------------------------------------------

### The place of the reallocate or deallocate call the running thread is in, with the heap check on.
@threadlocal
@target(os: not "none")
@heap_check("on")
secret global CALL_PLACE: &@SourceLocation

### The place of the reallocate or deallocate call in progress, on a target without an operating system.
@target(os: "none")
@heap_check("on")
secret global CALL_PLACE: &@SourceLocation

### Notes `place` as the place of the memory manager call in progress (null when it's over).
### :param place: The call's place, or null.
@inline
@heap_check("on")
secret routine note_call_place(place: @SourceLocation) -> None
    CALL_PLACE.poke(place)
    return

### Nothing to note with the heap check off.
### :param place: The call's place, or null.
@inline
@heap_check("off")
secret routine note_call_place(place: @SourceLocation) -> None
    return

### With the heap check on, where the `reallocate` or `deallocate` call that is running this thread's memory manager
### callback was made: the caller's line, for a memory manager that crashes on a mistake (the checked heap's block freed
### twice). Null outside such a call, and always null with the heap check off.
### :returns: The place, or null.
@heap_check("on")
routine memory_manager_call_place() -> @SourceLocation
    place: @SourceLocation = CALL_PLACE.peek()
    return place

### Always null with the heap check off: the place isn't noted then.
### :returns: The place, or null.
@heap_check("off")
routine memory_manager_call_place() -> @SourceLocation
    return .hollow()

### Copies `count` values of `T` from `src` to the values at this address, as raw bytes (`llvm.memcpy`):
### `dst.copy(src, count)`.
### :typeparam T: The element type.
### :param src: The first value to read.
### :param count: The number of values of `T` to copy.
### :throws: Crashes with `IntegerOverflowError` when the byte size does not fit in U64.
### :note: The ranges must not overlap, and no destructor or copy hook runs.
routine PointedCell[T].copy(src: Me, count: U64) -> None
    elem_size: U64 = sizeof[T]()
    size: U64 = elem_size * count
    raw_dst: Addr = me
    raw_src: Addr = src
    copy_bytes(dst: raw_dst, src: raw_src, bytes: size)
    return

### Copies `count` values of `T` from this address to `dst`: `dst.copy(me, count)` read from the source's side,
### `src.copy_into(dst, count)`.
### :typeparam T: The element type.
### :param dst: The first value to write to.
### :param count: The number of values of `T` to copy.
### :throws: Crashes with `IntegerOverflowError` when the byte size does not fit in U64.
### :note: The ranges must not overlap, and no destructor or copy hook runs.
routine PointedCell[T].copy_into(dst: Me, count: U64) -> None
    elem_size: U64 = sizeof[T]()
    size: U64 = elem_size * count
    raw_dst: Addr = dst
    raw_src: Addr = me
    copy_bytes(dst: raw_dst, src: raw_src, bytes: size)
    return

# llvm.memcpy, so the memory layer needs no libc: without an operating system, Freestanding.te supplies memcpy.
### Copies `bytes` bytes from `src` to `dst` with `llvm.memcpy`.
### :param dst: The destination address.
### :param src: The source address.
### :param bytes: The number of bytes to copy.
### :note: The ranges must not overlap.
@external("llvm")
@template("call void @llvm.memcpy.p0.p0.i64(ptr {dst}, ptr {src}, i64 {bytes}, i1 false)")
secret routine copy_bytes(dst: Addr, src: Addr, bytes: U64) -> None

# ============================================================
# C strings (Standard/System/CSubSystem/CStr.te)
# ============================================================

### A NUL-terminated copy of some text, for C, in memory from a memory manager: `text.to[CStr](manager)`. It lends a
### `CStrView` with `to[CStrView]()` and gives the memory back when dropped.
entity CStr
    storage: BorrowedSlice[CChar]

### A copy of some text in wide characters ending with a 0, for C, in memory from a memory manager:
### `text.to[CWStr](manager)`. It lends a `CWStrView` with `to[CWStrView]()` and gives the memory back when dropped.
entity CWStr
    storage: BorrowedSlice[CWChar]

### Makes a `CStr` from its fields.
### :param storage: Its `storage`.
### :returns: The new value.
common routine CStr.construct(storage: BorrowedSlice[CChar]) -> Me
    return Me(storage: steal storage)

### Makes a `CWStr` from its fields.
### :param storage: Its `storage`.
### :returns: The new value.
common routine CWStr.construct(storage: BorrowedSlice[CWChar]) -> Me
    return Me(storage: steal storage)

### The text, borrowed: valid until it is dropped.
### :returns: The NUL-terminated text.
routine @CStr.to[CStrView]() -> CStrView
    text: CStrView = me.storage.data.peek().to[CStrView]()
    return text

### The text, borrowed: valid until it is dropped.
### :returns: The 0-terminated wide text.
routine @CWStr.to[CWStrView]() -> CWStrView
    text: CWStrView = me.storage.data.peek().to[CWStrView]()
    return text

### Copies me into memory from manager and ends it with a NUL: an {CStr}, dropped when done.
### :param manager: The memory manager the copy's memory comes from.
### :returns: The NUL-terminated copy.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the memory manager fails.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates a copy")
routine @[Byte].to[CStr](manager: @MemoryManager) -> CStr
    count: U64 = me.count
    buffer: &[CChar] <- BorrowedSlice[CChar].construct(count: count + 1, manager: manager)
    buffer.data.peek().to[@Byte]().copy(src: me.data, count)
    buffer.data.peek().stride(count).poke(0)
    return CStr(storage: steal buffer)

### Converts me to wide characters in memory from manager, ending with a 0: UTF-16 on Windows, UTF-32 elsewhere. An
### {CWStr}, dropped when done. A UTF-8 byte count bounds both unit counts, so one allocation is enough.
### :param manager: The memory manager the copy's memory comes from.
### :returns: The 0-terminated wide copy.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the memory manager fails.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates a copy")
routine @[Byte].to[CWStr](manager: @MemoryManager) -> CWStr
    storage: &[CWChar] <- BorrowedSlice[CWChar].construct(count: me.count + 1, manager: manager)
    units: @CWChar = storage.data.peek()
    iter: &BytesCharsEmitter <- me.chars()
    n: &U64 <- 0

    loop
        c: Optional[Char] = try iter.emit()
        when c
            is Char as char =>
                count: U64 = encode_cwchar(char, out: units.stride(n.load()))
                (n.load() + count).poke_into(n)
                continue
            is None => break
    units.stride(n.load()).poke(0)
    return CWStr(storage: steal storage)

### Writes `char` at `out` as UTF-16, one unit or a surrogate pair, and returns
### how many units it wrote.
### :param char: The character to encode.
### :param out: Where the units go (room for 2 on Windows, 1 elsewhere).
### :returns: The number of units written.
@target(os: "windows")
secret routine encode_cwchar(char: Char, out: @CWChar) -> U64
    cp: U32 = char.to[U32]()

    if cp < 0x10000
        out.poke(cp.to[U16]())
        return 1
    v: U32 = cp - 0x10000
    high_unit: U32 = (v >> 10) + 0xD800
    out.poke(high_unit.to[U16]())
    low_unit: U32 = (v & 0x3FF) + 0xDC00
    out.stride(1).poke(low_unit.to[U16]())
    return 2

### Writes `char` at `out` as one UTF-32 unit. CWChar is S32 on some targets and U32 on others (ARM and AArch64 outside
### Apple). The code point's bits are the same either way.
### :param char: The character to encode.
### :param out: Where the units go (room for 2 on Windows, 1 elsewhere).
### :returns: The number of units written.
@target(os: not "windows")
secret routine encode_cwchar(char: Char, out: @CWChar) -> U64
    unit: @U32 = out.to[@U32]()
    unit.poke(char.to[U32]())
    return 1
